<feed xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><title>DW - English for Africa</title><subtitle type="html">News, Analysis and Service from Germany and Europe - in 30 Languages</subtitle><id>https://rss.dw.com/xml/rss-en-africa</id><updated>2026-10-30T06:02:35Z</updated><author><name>rssync</name></author><link rel="self" href="rss-en-africa" type="application/atom+xml" /><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.dw.com/s-12756?maca=en-rss-en-africa-8291-xml-mrss" /><link rel="via" href="https://rss.dw.com/xml/rss-en-africa" type="application/rss+xml" /><entry><title>Human trafficking victims stranded in Guinea-Bissau</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:b40c3cff8aebde971c3e21571596b51e98f331c71c563e73c5d2458fb0992ae8</id><updated>2026-10-30T06:02:35Z</updated><published>2026-10-29T14:20:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/human-trafficking-victims-stranded-in-guinea-bissau/a-79785558--af9688fcc3e2.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/human-trafficking-victims-stranded-in-guinea-bissau/a-79785558?maca=en-rss-en-africa-8291-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Dozens of West Africans lured to Guinea-Bissau with promises of jobs and migration to Europe say they were exploited by a suspected trafficking network. Now many cannot afford to return home.</summary><category term="World" /></entry><entry><title>One year on, families seek justice after election violence</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:2bea710a1f953722b357ece6bde9399a10f5879df2430f888e1fa59e309f8913</id><updated>2026-10-30T06:02:35Z</updated><published>2026-10-28T16:30:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/one-year-on-families-seek-justice-after-election-violence/a-79779105--19ee0afa750b.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/one-year-on-families-seek-justice-after-election-violence/a-79779105?maca=en-rss-en-africa-8291-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">One year after Tanzania's deadly post-election violence, families are still seeking answers. A government inquiry recorded 518 deaths. DW research has documented apparent use of excessive force by security forces.</summary><category term="World" /></entry><entry><title>Africa's first Youth Olympics open in Senegal amid hope and scrutiny</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:97d9c27bc25dd2b31c66d2159d24b5f67c76be07c9495d97a048bb6299f96ab3</id><updated>2026-10-30T06:02:35Z</updated><published>2026-10-28T09:08:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/africa-s-first-youth-olympics-open-in-senegal-amid-hope-and-scrutiny/a-79760478--c3155dbcff43.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/africa-s-first-youth-olympics-open-in-senegal-amid-hope-and-scrutiny/a-79760478?maca=en-rss-en-africa-8291-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Senegal is hosting Africa's first Youth Olympics, with 2,700 young athletes competing in Dakar, Diamniadio and Saly. The Games bring pride and hope, but also questions over cost, rights and legacy.</summary><category term="Sports" /></entry></feed>
